---
// A single take, listed but not yet playable in this task — `/takes/[id]`
// is Task 6's own detail page. This is a domain-specific composite (a Take
// + its instruments + the song/event context it's being listed under)
// rather than a generic primitive, so it lives here rather than in
// packages/ui — see the boundary rule in task-5-report.md.
//
// --- Variant design (Task 6, replacing Task 5's two ad-hoc boolean-ish
// props) -----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `context` is a discriminated union on `kind`, not a pair of optional
// `event`/`song` props a caller could set both-or-neither of by accident:
//
//   - "song"  — listed under a song page (`event` given): the row's own
//     link is the recorded date (this list is already scoped to one song
//     and ordered chronologically), plus a second link to the event.
//   - "event" — listed under an event page (`song` given): the row's own
//     link is the song title (that page's whole point is "what was
//     played"); no second link, since the page itself IS the event.
//   - "full"  — listed somewhere that is scoped to neither a single song
//     nor a single event (Task 6 adds three such places: `/search`, the
//     home page's "needs your vote", and its favorited-takes list): the
//     row needs the song title AND a recorded date AND a link to the
//     event, because none of those is implied by the page it's on.
//
// A caller can only construct one shape per `kind` — no "song and event
// both given", no "neither given", which the old `event?`/`song?` pair
// allowed silently (and which would have rendered a nonsensical row: two
// links reading the same thing, or a date-only row with no way to reach
// what was played).
//
// Two links reading the same text but pointing at different places would
// fail WCAG 2.4.4 (link purpose from text alone) — the second link is
// always phrased as "View this rehearsal/concert/session", never a repeat
// of the take's own label.
//
// --- The seam for increments 4 and 5 (both now filled) -----------------
//
// Two named slots are the extension points increments 4 and 5 live in —
// NOT a fourth prop each, and not a fork of this component:
//
//   - `leading`  — increment 4's play/pause affordance, rendered before the
//     take's own label. Filled with `PlayToggleButton` when `playableAssetId`
//     is given; omitted entirely (an empty `<slot>` emits no markup at all)
//     when it isn't — satisfying the brief's "no fake play affordance" rule
//     by construction, not by a conditional a caller has to remember.
//   - `trailing` — the numbers column. It HELD the vote control until this
//     row was looked at across all six pages that render it: keeper /
//     not-a-keeper appeared everywhere, including on takes already decided,
//     and two chunky buttons out-shouted the song title on every row. Voting
//     now lives on `/takes/[id]` alone — a vote cast without playing the take
//     is not worth much, and the page where you play it is the page where you
//     decide. The slot stays for callers that want something here; its default
//     content is now the instruments and the duration, which read as columns
//     down a list rather than as more words on the take's own line.
//
// Both slots default to that content rather than requiring every caller to
// pass a `<Fragment slot="...">` — a caller only needs to opt OUT (an
// explicit empty `<Fragment slot="trailing" />`) in the one case that needs
// to, never opt in. Neither slot needed a new prop shape, a fork, or a
// change to `context` to hold two real controls — see task-8-report.md's
// "did the TakeRow slots hold" section for the one place they didn't quite
// (the favorite mark below, which was never a slot to begin with).
//
// `TakeRowContext` itself (and its two ref types) lives in the sibling
// `take-row-context.ts`, not exported from here — see that file's header
// comment for why (an Astro server-build compiler bug, not a style choice).
//
// --- `transitionName` (Task 6 review round 1, F1) --------------------
//
// Every row used to hardcode `transition:name={`take-${take.id}`}`. That's
// fine as long as a given take id is rendered at most once per page — but
// `/` and `/me` both list takes across more than one section (favorites,
// recent events, needs-your-vote / favorites, votes), and a take that's
// both a favorite AND e.g. unvoted lands in two of them at once. Two
// elements sharing one `view-transition-name` isn't a warning — per the
// CSS View Transitions spec it silently aborts the transition for the
// WHOLE page, not just those two elements. `transitionName` lets the
// caller de-duplicate: omit it (or pass `undefined`) for the normal case
// and it defaults to `take-${take.id}`; pass `null` to suppress the
// attribute entirely for a take id the same page render already used once.
//
// --- `favorited` (Task 6 review round 1, F4; interactive since Task 8) --
//
// Gold's only prior use was a decorative star next to a "Your favorites"
// heading that already said the word "favorites" — no information. This
// prop drives a per-row star instead, filled when THIS row's take is one
// of the current member's favorites — real, row-specific information on
// pages that mix favorited and non-favorited takes (`/`'s
// recent-events/needs-your-vote sections, `/search`, `/takes/[id]`'s hero).
//
// Task 8 turns that static marker into `FavoriteToggle` (a real control,
// always rendered — an unfavorited row now shows an outline star, not
// nothing). This did NOT need a third named slot: the marker was never a
// slot in the first place (unlike `leading`/`trailing`), just markup
// TakeRow always owned and conditionally showed — swapping its innards
// from a `<span>` to a `<form>+<button>` is an internal change, not a
// structural one. It sits next to the label as a sibling of the `<a>`
// (never nested inside it — a `<form>` inside an `<a>` is invalid HTML),
// which is exactly the current markup shape.
//
// --- `data-take-id` (Task 6, Fix round 3) ------------------------------
//
// The link that carries `transition:name` also carries `data-take-id`, a
// plain hook for `TakeTransitionRetarget.astro`'s click-delegated script
// (imported only by the pages that can render the same take twice — see
// that component's own header comment). It has no effect at all without
// that script: with JS off, this attribute just sits there unused and
// round 1's first-occurrence-wins naming is exactly what still happens.
import type { instrumentsRepo, takesRepo } from "@bandplate/db";
import {
  eventKindLabel,
  eventLabel,
  formatDuration,
  formatShortDate,
  takeNote,
} from "../server/format.js";
import FavoriteToggle from "./FavoriteToggle.astro";
import InstrumentIcon from "./InstrumentIcon.astro";
import PlayToggleButton from "./PlayToggleButton.astro";
import type { TakeRowContext } from "./take-row-context.js";

export interface Props {
  take: takesRepo.Take;
  instruments: instrumentsRepo.Instrument[];
  context: TakeRowContext;
  /** `undefined` (the default, when omitted) → `take-${take.id}`. `null` →
   *  no `transition:name` at all. A caller listing the same take id more
   *  than once on one page must pass `null` for every occurrence after the
   *  first — see this file's header comment. */
  transitionName?: string | null;
  /** Shows the gold favorite marker next to the take's label. Defaults to
   *  `false` — see this file's header comment. */
  favorited?: boolean;
  /**
   * Increment 4's play control (see this file's header comment on the
   * `leading` slot) — the id of this take's ready master asset, when it
   * has one. `undefined` (the default) renders NO play control at all,
   * not a disabled one: the brief is explicit that a take with no
   * playable asset gets nothing, and every caller already has to compute
   * this (or not) per take, so there's no correct default other than
   * "omit it".
   */
  playableAssetId?: string;
}

const {
  take,
  instruments,
  context,
  transitionName,
  favorited = false,
  playableAssetId,
} = Astro.props;
const resolvedTransitionName =
  transitionName === null ? undefined : (transitionName ?? `take-${take.id}`);

const event = context.kind !== "event" ? context.event : undefined;
const song = context.kind !== "song" ? context.song : undefined;

// "song" mode's own list is already chronological — the date IS the thing
// that orients a reader there, never suppressed just because the take also
// has a label. "event" and "full" mode both use the song title instead,
// since "what was played" (not "when") is what those lists are for. A
// label (when present) renders as a sublabel underneath in every mode,
// never as a replacement for the primary link text.
const primaryLabel = song ? song.title : formatShortDate(take.recordedAt);
// "full" mode alone needs an explicit date line — it's the one context
// where the date isn't implied by the page (a song page's rows already
// linger under that song; an event page's rows already share one date).
const showDate = context.kind === "full";
// The play control's own accessible subtitle (announced by the player,
// not shown visually here — the row's own markup already shows this same
// information) — mirrors whichever of date/label this row doesn't already
// use as its primary link text.
const playSubtitle = song ? (take.label ?? formatShortDate(take.recordedAt)) : (take.label ?? "");
// The event's NAME, and only its name — "Live at The Attic — The Attic" tells
// you which event this was, where "View this rehearsal" told you only that one
// existed. Empty when the event has neither title nor venue, which is the
// common case for a seeded rehearsal: the kind badge beside the title already
// says WHAT it was, so a name line that could only repeat that word is simply
// not rendered rather than filled with a fallback.
const eventName = event
  ? eventLabel({ title: event.title ?? null, venue: event.venue ?? null })
  : "";
const eventKind = event ? eventKindLabel(event.kind) : "";
const noteText = takeNote(take.label, eventKind);
---

<div class="bp-take-row">
  <slot name="leading">
    {
      playableAssetId ? (
        <PlayToggleButton
          takeId={take.id}
          assetId={playableAssetId}
          title={primaryLabel}
          subtitle={playSubtitle}
        />
      ) : (
        /* A take with no ready asset gets NO play affordance — a disabled-looking
           play button is the fake affordance the brief forbids. It gets a plate:
           a ring with a still centre, aria-hidden, no role, no tabstop. A bullet,
           not a control, so the list edge stays straight while an upload is in
           flight. */
        <span class="bp-take-plate" aria-hidden="true" />
      )
    }
  </slot>
  <div class="bp-take-row-main">
    {/* FOUR lines, in this order, on EVERY page that renders this row — the
        structure does not branch on `context`. A line is present when its data
        is and absent when it isn't; nothing reorders, so a take read on the
        home page, in search results, on a song page and on an event page is
        read the same way each time. That was the actual complaint behind
        several rounds of this: the same facts kept landing in different places
        (and wrapping differently) depending on how long a venue name was.

          1. title + kind badge + favourite
          2. the event's name, when it has one
          3. the take's own note, when it has one
          4. date (where the page doesn't already imply it) + instruments
    */}
    <div class="bp-take-row-line">
      <a
        href={`/takes/${take.id}`}
        class="bp-take-label"
        transition:name={resolvedTransitionName}
        data-take-id={take.id}
      >
        {primaryLabel}
      </a>
      {eventKind && <span class="bp-badge bp-badge-kind">{eventKind}</span>}
      <span class="bp-take-favorite-mark">
        <FavoriteToggle
          targetType="take"
          targetId={take.id}
          favorited={favorited}
          label={primaryLabel}
        />
      </span>
    </div>
    {event && eventName && (
      <div class="bp-take-meta bp-take-eventline">
        <a href={`/events/${event.id}`} class="bp-take-event-link">
          {eventName}
        </a>
      </div>
    )}
    {noteText && <div class="bp-take-meta bp-take-note">{noteText}</div>}
    {(showDate || instruments.length > 0) && (
      <div class="bp-take-meta bp-take-subline">
        {showDate && <span>{formatShortDate(take.recordedAt)}</span>}
        {instruments.length > 0 && (
          <span
            class="bp-take-instruments"
            role="img"
            aria-label={`Instruments: ${instruments.map((i) => i.label).join(", ")}`}
          >
            {instruments.map((instrument) => (
              <InstrumentIcon icon={instrument.icon} label={instrument.label} />
            ))}
          </span>
        )}
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
  {/* The trailing column is where the vote control used to be. Instruments moved
      into it so they read as a COLUMN down the list rather than as more words on
      the take's own line — the same reason the duration lives here. Voting is on
      `/takes/[id]` only: a vote cast without playing the take is not worth much,
      and putting the control on all six pages that render this row was what made
      it shout. Callers that want something here still have the slot. */}
  {/* The trailing column is the duration and nothing else. Instruments were
      briefly here — they read as a column, which was the point — but on a row
      that already carries a date, a badge and an event they crowded the right
      edge at any width narrower than a full page. They sit on the second line
      now, beside the event, where the row has room. */}
  <div class="bp-take-row-trailing">
    <slot name="trailing" />
    <span class="bp-take-row-duration">{formatDuration(take.durationMs)}</span>
  </div>
</div>
